{
  "image_name": "2a21969fa96b8d2db7d7fd2e67de25ec.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several ways the m6A RNA modification pathway controls gene expression from the nucleus to the cytoplasm. In the nucleus, a writer complex containing METTL3, METTL14, and WTAP adds m6A marks to mRNA, particularly around stop-codon regions, while the erasers FTO and ALKBH5 remove them. Modified RNAs then pass through the nuclear pore and are used differently in the cytoplasm depending on which reader proteins bind.\n\nOne prominent outcome is enhanced translation: METTL3 appears to promote ribosome-associated translation, with eIF3 shown as part of this process, and EGFR given as an example target. Another branch shows m6A participating in pri-miRNA processing, producing pre-miRNAs. The figure also indicates a chromatin-associated role for METTL3 near promoter elements such as the CAAT and TATA boxes, where it seems linked to RNA polymerase II and increased translation of genes including SP1 and SP2.\n\nA separate panel highlights reader proteins that determine RNA fate after m6A marking. YTHDF2 is associated with RNA decay, IGF2BP1 and YTHDF1 with RNA stability, and YTHDC2 with translation. Example downstream targets include cancer-related transcripts such as MYC, SOX2, FOXM1, and HIF1A, emphasizing that m6A can influence degradation, stability, or protein production depending on context.",
